In a C program, you can use the `exit()` function to terminate the program explicitly. The `exit()` function takes an integer argument, which represents the exit status of the program. If you don't provide an argument, the default exit status is 0.

Here's an example of how to use `exit()` to terminate a C program:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    // Your code here

    // Terminate the program with an exit status of 1
    exit(1);
}
```

If you want to terminate the program due to an error or exception, you can call `exit()` directly without returning from the current function. For example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

void my_function(void) {
    // Your code here

    // Terminate the program with an exit status of 1
    exit(1);
}
```

If you don't call `exit()` explicitly, the program will continue executing until it reaches the end of the `main()` function, at which point it will automatically terminate with an exit status of 0.
